#!/bin/sh
# script/icount: the instruction-count instrument (milestone 78).
#
#   script/icount                    both ISAs
#   script/icount --arch aarch64     one leg
#   script/icount --arch riscv64
#
# Boots a `--features icount` kernel under `-icount shift=0,sleep=off`, where QEMU's virtual clock
# advances by exactly one nanosecond per guest instruction retired and by nothing else. The guest
# asserts two claims that no wall-clock test on either ISA can make, because from inside the guest a
# slow handler and a descheduled emulator are the same observation:
#
#   1. the timer fired at the deadline the kernel armed (on riscv64, that SBI was armed with the
#      DEADLINE word rather than with something else that keeps the array looking right), and
#   2. the handler takes fewer than N instructions, deadline to re-armed.
#
# It also asserts zero missed ticks, which is only assertable here: the miss taxonomy on both ISAs
# exists to tell a slow handler from a descheduled emulator, and virtual time has no deschedules.
#
# **Not part of script/test, deliberately**, and the reason is not that it is slow: an identical
# compute-bound boot measured the same either way. It is that all vCPUs share ONE virtual clock under
# -icount, so the instrument is single-hart and a suite run here would stop proving every cross-core
# property it exists for; and that a clock-bound wait costs guest instructions rather than host time.
# This is its own boot for the same reason script/bench is. See
# design/roadmap/78-load-sensitive-assertions.md and notes/instruction-clock.md.
#
# Name: provisional. calef names the scripts, the crates and the programs (AGENTS.md); this one
# borrows QEMU's own flag, which is the term of art a reader already knows.

set -e
cd "$(dirname "$0")/.."
exec cargo xtask icount "$@"
